{"created": "2026-10-17T14:21:25.686440+00:00", "modified": "2026-10-17T14:21:25.835769+00:00", "id": 23437544, "conceptrecid": "23437543", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23437544", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.23437543", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23437544", "metadata": {"title": "Reproduction package: How Valid Are Reported Android Malware Detection Scores? Resampling Leakage, Explanation Stability, and Add-Only Feature-Space Evasion on CICMalDroid2020", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23437544", "publication_date": "2026-10-17", "description": "<p>Reproduction package for the paper \"How Valid Are Reported Android Malware Detection Scores? A Study of Resampling Leakage, Explanation Stability, and Add-Only Feature-Space Evasion on CICMalDroid2020.\"</p>\n<p>Contents: Python experiment scripts (honest detection baseline, SMOTE-before-split leakage analysis, per-category SHAP, add-only feature-space evasion with an adversarial-augmentation defence, and an XGBoost replication), all result CSV logs, the fixed stratified 70/30 split indices (seed 42) for exact reproduction, publication figures, and the manuscript LaTeX source and references.</p>\n<p>The CICMalDroid2020 dynamic feature file (feature_vectors_syscallsbinders_frequency_5_Cat.csv; 11,598 samples x 470 system-call/binder frequency features; classes 1=Adware, 2=Banking, 3=SMS, 4=Riskware, 5=Benign) is NOT redistributed here and must be obtained from the Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity (UNB): https://www.unb.ca/cic/datasets/maldroid-2020.html</p>\n<p>Environment: Python 3.11 with scikit-learn, xgboost, imbalanced-learn, shap, pandas, numpy, matplotlib. Fixed random seed = 42 throughout. See README_reproduce.txt for run instructions.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Sugiantoro, Bambang", "affiliation": "Sunan Kalijaga State Islamic University Yogyakarta", "orcid": "0000-0002-7548-8122"}, {"name": "Hafizuddin, Faiz Akhmad", "affiliation": "Sunan Kalijaga State Islamic University Yogyakar